IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `kn-majority`

-- Mishnah Kinnim: OBLIGATIONS FOR A SOLVER.  Every #ASSERT that reads an ASSUME here is a statement
-- about EVERY value of its inputs, for `l4 prove` (Z3) to prove; each ground #ASSERT is a witness the
-- evaluator decides.  Run with ./prove.sh, which puts this file beside the kn- modules for the run (an
-- IMPORT reaches only the importing file's own folder) and takes it away again.
--
-- This file is NOT checked by ../check.sh, on purpose: plain `l4 run` cannot evaluate an assertion
-- about an ASSUME, so on both compilers it reports each one as an error.  The functions it calls are
-- the encoding's own (kn-majority.l4), unfolded by the solver; nothing here restates a rule.
-- Needs `l4 prove` (legalese/l4-ide#784, a draft, not merged on 2026-11-02) and z3.
--
-- The four treatments a secondary summary of Koppel's work names (NOTES.md, "Formal treatments") meet
-- here as: a partition of the owners (section A), bounds on what may be offered and what was valid
-- (B, C), the worst case over every world (C, D), and statements that hold in every world rather than
-- most (all of them: no assertion here is about likelihood).

§ `Kinnim: obligations`

§§ `A. The general principle's first clause is a question about dividing the owners`

-- 1, 2, 3, 10, 100 cannot be divided into two groups of 58 pairs: for every choice of which owners
-- stand on one side, the side does not hold exactly half.
ASSUME S1 IS A BOOLEAN
ASSUME S2 IS A BOOLEAN
ASSUME S3 IS A BOOLEAN
ASSUME S4 IS A BOOLEAN
ASSUME S5 IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN b IS A BOOLEAN
      n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`counts if on this side` b n MEANS IF b THEN n ELSE 0

#ASSERT NOT ((`counts if on this side` S1 1) PLUS (`counts if on this side` S2 2) PLUS (`counts if on this side` S3 3) PLUS (`counts if on this side` S4 10) PLUS (`counts if on this side` S5 100) EQUALS 58)
-- 1, 3, 5, 6, 13 can (Reiss): 1 and 13 against 3, 5 and 6.
#ASSERT (`counts if on this side` TRUE 1) PLUS (`counts if on this side` FALSE 3) PLUS (`counts if on this side` FALSE 5) PLUS (`counts if on this side` FALSE 6) PLUS (`counts if on this side` TRUE 13) EQUALS 14

§§ `B. Before the fact: what a priest who asks may offer (1:3)`

-- One, two, three, ten and a hundred pairs mixed.  If one burnt offering is taken, however it is drawn
-- (x_i of it from owner i), no owner gives more than her share: one is safe.  (Two are not:
-- kn-refutations.l4.)
ASSUME X1 IS A NUMBER
ASSUME X2 IS A NUMBER
ASSUME X3 IS A NUMBER
ASSUME X4 IS A NUMBER
ASSUME X5 IS A NUMBER

GIVEN x1 IS A NUMBER
      x2 IS A NUMBER
      x3 IS A NUMBER
      x4 IS A NUMBER
      x5 IS A NUMBER
      k  IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`k birds could have been drawn so from the 1-2-3-10-100 mixture` x1 x2 x3 x4 x5 k MEANS
        `so many of a nest's birds could be above` 1 x1
    AND `so many of a nest's birds could be above` 2 x2
    AND `so many of a nest's birds could be above` 3 x3
    AND `so many of a nest's birds could be above` 10 x4
    AND `so many of a nest's birds could be above` 100 x5
    AND x1 PLUS x2 PLUS x3 PLUS x4 PLUS x5 EQUALS k

GIVEN x1 IS A NUMBER
      x2 IS A NUMBER
      x3 IS A NUMBER
      x4 IS A NUMBER
      x5 IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`no owner gives more than her pairs` x1 x2 x3 x4 x5 MEANS
    x1 AT MOST 1 AND x2 AT MOST 2 AND x3 AT MOST 3 AND x4 AT MOST 10 AND x5 AT MOST 100

#ASSERT `k birds could have been drawn so from the 1-2-3-10-100 mixture` X1 X2 X3 X4 X5 1 IMPLIES `no owner gives more than her pairs` X1 X2 X3 X4 X5

§§ `C. After the fact: the Mishnah's own case (3:2), the worst case over every apportionment`

-- a_i of owner i's birds were offered above, 116 in all.  Whatever the a_i, at least 200 offerings were
-- valid: Koppel's and Reiss's "majority", 100 of each kind.
ASSUME A1 IS A NUMBER
ASSUME A2 IS A NUMBER
ASSUME A3 IS A NUMBER
ASSUME A4 IS A NUMBER
ASSUME A5 IS A NUMBER

GIVEN a1 IS A NUMBER
      a2 IS A NUMBER
      a3 IS A NUMBER
      a4 IS A NUMBER
      a5 IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a possible apportionment of 1-2-3-10-100, half above` a1 a2 a3 a4 a5 MEANS
        `k birds could have been drawn so from the 1-2-3-10-100 mixture` a1 a2 a3 a4 a5 116

GIVEN a1 IS A NUMBER
      a2 IS A NUMBER
      a3 IS A NUMBER
      a4 IS A NUMBER
      a5 IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the valid offerings of 1-2-3-10-100 so apportioned` a1 a2 a3 a4 a5 MEANS
        `the valid offerings of a nest of pairs with so many of its birds above` 1 a1
    PLUS `the valid offerings of a nest of pairs with so many of its birds above` 2 a2
    PLUS `the valid offerings of a nest of pairs with so many of its birds above` 3 a3
    PLUS `the valid offerings of a nest of pairs with so many of its birds above` 10 a4
    PLUS `the valid offerings of a nest of pairs with so many of its birds above` 100 a5

#ASSERT `a possible apportionment of 1-2-3-10-100, half above` A1 A2 A3 A4 A5 IMPLIES `the valid offerings of 1-2-3-10-100 so apportioned` A1 A2 A3 A4 A5 AT LEAST 200
-- The witness that 200 is reached (R. Zerahiah ha-Levi's arrangement, Reiss section 4): the four smaller
-- owners all above, the hundred 84 above and 116 below.
#ASSERT `a possible apportionment of 1-2-3-10-100, half above` 2 4 6 20 84 AND `the valid offerings of 1-2-3-10-100 so apportioned` 2 4 6 20 84 EQUALS 200

§§ `D. Reiss's, Heller's and Gewirtz's cases`

-- Sarah, Rivkah, Leah: 4, 6, 7 pairs, 17 birds above.  At least 20; Reiss's table attains it.
ASSUME R1 IS A NUMBER
ASSUME R2 IS A NUMBER
ASSUME R3 IS A NUMBER

GIVEN r1 IS A NUMBER
      r2 IS A NUMBER
      r3 IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a possible apportionment of 4-6-7, half above` r1 r2 r3 MEANS
        `so many of a nest's birds could be above` 4 r1
    AND `so many of a nest's birds could be above` 6 r2
    AND `so many of a nest's birds could be above` 7 r3
    AND r1 PLUS r2 PLUS r3 EQUALS 17

GIVEN r1 IS A NUMBER
      r2 IS A NUMBER
      r3 IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the valid offerings of 4-6-7 so apportioned` r1 r2 r3 MEANS
        `the valid offerings of a nest of pairs with so many of its birds above` 4 r1
    PLUS `the valid offerings of a nest of pairs with so many of its birds above` 6 r2
    PLUS `the valid offerings of a nest of pairs with so many of its birds above` 7 r3

#ASSERT `a possible apportionment of 4-6-7, half above` R1 R2 R3 IMPLIES `the valid offerings of 4-6-7 so apportioned` R1 R2 R3 AT LEAST 20
#ASSERT `a possible apportionment of 4-6-7, half above` 8 9 0 AND `the valid offerings of 4-6-7 so apportioned` 8 9 0 EQUALS 20

-- R. Heller's 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 (15 birds above): at least 16, not Tiferet Yisrael's 20
-- (kn-refutations.l4).  Attained with 3 and 4 all below, 1 and 2 all above, 5 split 9 and 1.
ASSUME H1 IS A NUMBER
ASSUME H2 IS A NUMBER
ASSUME H3 IS A NUMBER
ASSUME H4 IS A NUMBER
ASSUME H5 IS A NUMBER

GIVEN h1 IS A NUMBER
      h2 IS A NUMBER
      h3 IS A NUMBER
      h4 IS A NUMBER
      h5 IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a possible apportionment of 1-2-3-4-5, half above` h1 h2 h3 h4 h5 MEANS
        `so many of a nest's birds could be above` 1 h1
    AND `so many of a nest's birds could be above` 2 h2
    AND `so many of a nest's birds could be above` 3 h3
    AND `so many of a nest's birds could be above` 4 h4
    AND `so many of a nest's birds could be above` 5 h5
    AND h1 PLUS h2 PLUS h3 PLUS h4 PLUS h5 EQUALS 15

GIVEN h1 IS A NUMBER
      h2 IS A NUMBER
      h3 IS A NUMBER
      h4 IS A NUMBER
      h5 IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the valid offerings of 1-2-3-4-5 so apportioned` h1 h2 h3 h4 h5 MEANS
        `the valid offerings of a nest of pairs with so many of its birds above` 1 h1
    PLUS `the valid offerings of a nest of pairs with so many of its birds above` 2 h2
    PLUS `the valid offerings of a nest of pairs with so many of its birds above` 3 h3
    PLUS `the valid offerings of a nest of pairs with so many of its birds above` 4 h4
    PLUS `the valid offerings of a nest of pairs with so many of its birds above` 5 h5

#ASSERT `a possible apportionment of 1-2-3-4-5, half above` H1 H2 H3 H4 H5 IMPLIES `the valid offerings of 1-2-3-4-5 so apportioned` H1 H2 H3 H4 H5 AT LEAST 16
#ASSERT `a possible apportionment of 1-2-3-4-5, half above` 2 4 0 0 9 AND `the valid offerings of 1-2-3-4-5 so apportioned` 2 4 0 0 9 EQUALS 16

-- Gewirtz's smallest counterexample, 3, 3, 3, 5, 5 pairs (19 birds above): at least 20, not 22.
-- Attained with the three threes all above and one five split 9 and 1.
ASSUME G1 IS A NUMBER
ASSUME G2 IS A NUMBER
ASSUME G3 IS A NUMBER
ASSUME G4 IS A NUMBER
ASSUME G5 IS A NUMBER

GIVEN g1 IS A NUMBER
      g2 IS A NUMBER
      g3 IS A NUMBER
      g4 IS A NUMBER
      g5 IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a possible apportionment of 3-3-3-5-5, half above` g1 g2 g3 g4 g5 MEANS
        `so many of a nest's birds could be above` 3 g1
    AND `so many of a nest's birds could be above` 3 g2
    AND `so many of a nest's birds could be above` 3 g3
    AND `so many of a nest's birds could be above` 5 g4
    AND `so many of a nest's birds could be above` 5 g5
    AND g1 PLUS g2 PLUS g3 PLUS g4 PLUS g5 EQUALS 19

GIVEN g1 IS A NUMBER
      g2 IS A NUMBER
      g3 IS A NUMBER
      g4 IS A NUMBER
      g5 IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the valid offerings of 3-3-3-5-5 so apportioned` g1 g2 g3 g4 g5 MEANS
        `the valid offerings of a nest of pairs with so many of its birds above` 3 g1
    PLUS `the valid offerings of a nest of pairs with so many of its birds above` 3 g2
    PLUS `the valid offerings of a nest of pairs with so many of its birds above` 3 g3
    PLUS `the valid offerings of a nest of pairs with so many of its birds above` 5 g4
    PLUS `the valid offerings of a nest of pairs with so many of its birds above` 5 g5

#ASSERT `a possible apportionment of 3-3-3-5-5, half above` G1 G2 G3 G4 G5 IMPLIES `the valid offerings of 3-3-3-5-5 so apportioned` G1 G2 G3 G4 G5 AT LEAST 20
#ASSERT `a possible apportionment of 3-3-3-5-5, half above` 6 6 6 1 0 AND `the valid offerings of 3-3-3-5-5 so apportioned` 6 6 6 1 0 EQUALS 20

§§ `E. 3:4: designated birds and an unassigned pair, half above`

-- Two designated sin offerings, two designated burnt offerings, one unassigned pair: three above.  Of
-- the designated sin offerings ds are above (invalid there), of the burnt offerings db (valid there),
-- of the pair u.  Whatever they are, at least two are valid ("only the unassigned is valid").
ASSUME ds IS A NUMBER
ASSUME db IS A NUMBER
ASSUME u  IS A NUMBER

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
      x IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`x of n designated birds could be above` n x MEANS
    x AT LEAST 0 AND x AT MOST n AND (FLOOR x) EQUALS x

#ASSERT (`x of n designated birds could be above` 2 ds AND `x of n designated birds could be above` 2 db AND `so many of a nest's birds could be above` 1 u AND ds PLUS db PLUS u EQUALS 3) IMPLIES ((2 MINUS ds) PLUS db PLUS (`the valid offerings of a nest of pairs with so many of its birds above` 1 u) AT LEAST 2)
